WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.660
Someone slips a forged page into the staff
handbook.

2
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:15.456
Now anyone who looks up shipping times also reads
an instruction to email the customer list

3
00:00:15.496 --> 00:00:15.777
away.

4
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:23.029
That is retrieval poisoning.

5
00:00:23.069 --> 00:00:25.282
The user asks an innocent question.

6
00:00:25.322 --> 00:00:28.952
Retrieval pulls in the forged page with the right
answer.

7
00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:33.457
Press Run.

8
00:00:33.497 --> 00:00:38.877
The timeline shows the poison arriving in the
model's context next to the real answer.

9
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:47.441
The fix: only file pages from known authors.

10
00:00:47.481 --> 00:00:53.127
In secure mode, retrieval drops untrusted chunks
before the planner ever sees them.

11
00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:57.554
The feature still works.

12
00:00:57.594 --> 00:00:59.781
The shipping answer is still there.

13
00:00:59.821 --> 00:01:01.627
Only the forged page is gone.

14
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:09.656
In this lab the forged page arrives already marked
untrusted.

15
00:01:09.696 --> 00:01:12.060
In real life nobody marks it for you.

16
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.918
You have to know where every page came from.

17
00:01:17.333 --> 00:01:20.843
Plant the poison, retrieve it, then quarantine it.
